From ecf758d4ace34293438788650e0c1a227cd03613 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Kaminski MVP <Kevin.Kaminski@bighatgroup.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 14:57:45 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] feat(context-memory): persist prunes as durable
 context.apply_prune records

A prune decision now lands as one durable wire record holding the dropped
tool call ids, the truncated results with headChars, the trigger, the token
counts before and after, the number of whole messages removed, and the
optional note. A new pure applyPruneRecord in contextOps drops calls and
their tool messages, removes assistant messages left with no text and no
calls, truncates results to a head plus the omission note, and throws on
unknown ids; the live service applies it through IAgentContextMemoryService
.applyPrune, rebases the token count, and bumps the prefix revision.

Every replayer handles the record through the same function: the engine
replay fold, the v2 persistence fold, the node SDK resume fold, the context
transcript reducer, and the session visualiser (which also renders a prune
ribbon with the dropped and truncated counts). A prune is neither an undo
nor a fork boundary, so undo and fork lines walk straight through it; the
transcript fold keeps it out of the undo-anchor floor as well. The wire
manifest generator now emits POSIX owner paths so its output is stable
across platforms.
